"""A twin of a customer specification exported from a requirements-management tool: the real document's structure, one for one, with
every name, value and sentence invented (a fictional greenhouse climate controller). It reproduces the layouts that defeated fixed rules:

- a deep running header grid (label and value rows, ~115 pt) on every page, whose lower rows sit inside the body area;
- a three-line notice rotated 90 degrees in the left margin of every page;
- headings at body size (level 1 bold uppercase, level 2 uppercase, levels 3-4 mixed case) found only through the bookmark outline;
- lowercase requirement codes with no separator (``abc_12``) in a hanging-indent column beside the requirement text;
- under each requirement, a trace label and its parent codes one per line, and Notes inside the requirement column or full width;
- lists inside requirements and Notes, requirements and lists that cross a page, requirements stated directly under a section before
  its subsections, sections whose whole body is "None." or "Not applicable.", requirements with no obligation word, uppercase product
  and standard tokens inside the text, and a 116-row traceability listing over several pages as back matter.

``build_twin_spec_pdf(out_dir)`` writes the PDF and returns ``(path, truth)``; ``truth`` is the tree the document states."""

